Expected settlement is 6.75 + 6.8 + 4.875 = 18.425, with a standard deviation of about 6.5.

Half of the counterparties know the exact settlement value, and noise traders are only about 6.5 wide. With these numbers the best half-spread works out to roughly 1.2 standard deviations, about 7.5 to 8. I'll quote around 18.4 ± 7.5.

{"bid": 10.9, "ask": 25.9}

Fair value is the sum of the three dice means: 5.75 + 5 + 5.25 = 16.0. The settlement standard deviation is about 6.6, roughly the same as the noise traders' spread.

I weighed noise-trader profit against losses to informed traders. Expected profit per side is highest at a half-spread of about 0.9 standard deviations, which is about 6. So I quote 10 / 22, centred on 16.

{"bid": 10.0, "ask": 22.0}

Before any cards are revealed, the expected sum of three cards is 3 × 7 = 21. The standard deviation of the sum is about 6.35, because the cards are drawn without replacement.

I compared expected profit per side for different half-spreads around 21. I weighed what I earn from noise traders (spread 4.8) against what I lose to the informed trader, who knows the exact sum. Profit is roughly flat between half-spreads of 4 and 5, so I'll use about 4.5.

{"bid": 16.5, "ask": 25.5}

Fair value is about 23.4, since the 10 cards average 7.8 and 3 are dealt. The quote 1 no-trade barely moves it because that quote was symmetric. The 3-card sum has a standard deviation of about 7.2, and noise traders scatter with a standard deviation of 5.4.

On a normal approximation, profit from noise traders roughly offsets losses to informed traders across most widths. The edge is thin and is best around a half-spread of 8 to 8.5. I'll keep the spread roughly as wide as last time and centred on 23.4.

{"bid": 15.2, "ask": 31.6}

Card 1 was a 12, so 12 cards remain. Their values sum to 41, which is about 3.42 per card. The expected sum of the two unrevealed cards is about 6.83, so fair value is about 18.8. The trading history barely changes this.

The two unrevealed cards together have a standard deviation of about 4.2. Noise traders' valuations are wide (standard deviation 8.9), so I can quote a fairly wide spread and still trade with them. I set the half-spread at roughly 3.7 around fair. I am short 1, so I shift the quotes up slightly to make buying a little more likely than selling.

{"bid": 15.2, "ask": 22.6}

Fair value is about 6.7. Given draw 1 is red, the posterior is proportional to R. I then weighted it by the chance of the three sells at low bids. Those sells push some extra weight onto R=1. The posterior mean comes out at roughly 6.7.

I'm long 3, so I'm skewing toward selling. The ask sits close to fair value and the bid sits well below it, which also guards against informed sellers.

{"bid": 4.5, "ask": 7.8}

Three blue draws make a low red count likely. The weight for each R is (11−R)(10−R)(9−R). Applied to the uniform prior, this gives E[R] = 1.6, so the fair value is about 16. The earlier trades nudge this to roughly 16–17. The buy at 50 slightly raises the chance of R≥6, and the no-trade at 49 slightly lowers it.

Informed traders are dangerous on both sides. The bid side is the bigger risk, since R≤1 has about a 57% chance. I'm also short 1, so I set a slightly generous bid to lean towards flattening, and keep a wide ask.

{"bid": 9, "ask": 28}

Fair value is 50, the average of red counts 0 to 10, times 10.

I compared spread widths by subtracting the expected loss to informed traders from the expected gain on noise traders. Expected profit per side was highest at a half-spread of about 38 to 40, roughly 6.1 per side.

{"bid": 11, "ask": 89}

Die 3 = 6, so the prior expected settlement is 5.75 + 5 + 6 = 16.75. The order flow points higher. In stage 1, counterparties bought at 19 and 20.5. In stage 2 they sold at 16, then didn't trade at 16 / 21. Weighting those trades by the chance each one came from an informed trader moves my estimate up to about 18.3.

The total of the two unrevealed dice is still very uncertain (standard deviation about 5), so I keep a wide spread. I'm short 1, so I lean the quote slightly upward.

{"bid": 15.5, "ask": 21}

Fair value is about 25.6. Each card averages 145/17 ≈ 8.53, so three cards sum to about 25.6 on average. The standard deviation of the three-card sum is about 5.5 (drawing without replacement shrinks it slightly).

I set a half-spread of roughly 3.2 around fair value. This trades off profit from noise traders, whose valuations also spread with SD 5.5, against losses to informed traders, who know the true sum.

{"bid": 22.4, "ask": 28.8}

Public fair value is about 10.9. The unconditional expected product is 12.25. The quote-1 no-trade and the quote-2 sale at 6 pull that down, since the sale makes a product of 5 more likely.

The product has a wide spread (standard deviation about 9), and 35% of counterparties are informed, so I keep the quote wide. I'm long 1, so I lean slightly toward selling.

{"bid": 6, "ask": 15}

Before any cards are shown, the expected maximum of the three cards is about 11.98. The sale at my bid of 10.8 lowers my estimate of fair value to about 11.4. That sale is somewhat more likely from an informed trader who knows the maximum is 10 or less.

I am long 1, so I skew my quote slightly lower to make selling easier than buying. I keep a reasonably wide spread because there is a 40% chance the maximum is 15. In that case informed traders will buy from me at my ask.

{"bid": 10.1, "ask": 12.6}

Expected settlement: the average heads probability is 0.3×0.2 + 0.4×0.5 + 0.3×0.7 = 0.47, so expected heads in 7 flips is 7 × 0.47 = 3.29. Settlement uncertainty is fairly wide, with a standard deviation of about 1.83.

Spread choice: I compared half-spreads of 1.5, 2 and 2.5. The informed trader's expected edge against me is small at these widths. Profit from noise traders is highest near a half-spread of 2. So I quote about 3.3 ± 2.

{"bid": 1.3, "ask": 5.3}

Draw 1 came up red, so the posterior on red balls is proportional to R. That alone gives a mean of 7. The stage-1 history adds information: two no-trades, then a counterparty sold to me at 2.8. That sell tilts weight toward low R, and folding it in brings the public fair value down to about 6.3.

I quote about 1.5 either side of that. I'm already long one, so I shade both sides slightly lower to encourage selling.

